{"type":"assistant","uuid":"463b32e8-310e-44d0-bec6-ff72b8d08171","session_id":"1516a499-6692-4325-86ae-bc8b46ab2a73","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"463b32e8-310e-44d0-bec6-ff72b8d08171","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full picture. The issue is clear:\n\nIn `TagsHelper.removeTag()`, the method uses `String.replaceAll()` which replaces **all** occurrences of a tag text in the title and content. When a user unchecks a tag checkbox, only that tag instance should be removed from the note, but all tags with the same text are removed.\n\nThe fix should change `replaceAll` to use `replaceFirst` (which only replaces the first occurrence) since each checkbox represents one tag instance in the note. Since `addTagToNote` returns one `Tag` per unique tag text being removed, using `replaceFirst` will remove just one occurrence per tag.\n\nWait, let me think about this more carefully. The `addTagToNote` method returns tags that should be removed. Each tag in `tagsToRemove` represents one unique tag that the user unchecked. The `retrieveTags` method returns a HashMap with tag text as key and count as value. So if a note has \"#test\" appearing 3 times, the HashMap would have `{\"#test\": 3}`. When the user unchecks the #test tag, `addTagToNote` would add one Tag object to `tagsToRemove`. Then `removeTag` should remove all 3 occurrences... wait, that doesn't seem right either.\n\n\nActually, I'm realizing the real issue: the dialog shows each tag once regardless of how many times it appears in the note, so unchecking it should only remove that single instance, not all occurrences. If a note has `#test`, `#testa`, and `#testb`, the dialog shows three items, and unchecking `#test` should only remove that one tag, leaving the others intact.\n\nThe problem is that `String.replaceAll()` replaces all occurrences of the string, so if there are multiple `#test` tags in the note, they'd all get deleted. The solution is to use `replaceFirst()` instead to remove just one occurrence. The `Tag` object contains the text (like `#test`), and when we retrieve tags from a note, we're building a HashMap of all unique tags found in the title and content. I see the issue now. The bug report shows a scenario where a note contains multiple tags like `#test`, `#testa`, and `#testb`. When the user unchecks the `#test` checkbox in the dialog, `addTagToNote` correctly identifies it for removal and returns a single Tag object for `#test`. The `removeTag` method then uses `String.replaceAll()`, which should remove all instances of `#test` from the note's content. But the bug occurs because the user only wanted to remove the checkbox selection, not all occurrences of that tag from the text.\n\nLooking at the bug report more carefully, the issue is that when a user unchecks `#test`, it's removing all tags containing \"test\" — including `#testa` and `#testb` — instead of just the exact `#test` tag. The `replaceAll` pattern is too broad and matches substrings it shouldn't. I see the issue now—the bug description mentions removing \"all tag contain 'test'\" which could mean either removing `#testa` and `#testb` as well, or just removing all instances of `#test`. Looking at the \"Expected behavior\" section which says `#testa` and `#testb` should remain, it seems the problem might be broader than just multiple occurrences of the same tag. The root cause appears to be in how `String.replaceAll()` handles the pattern matching, which could potentially be matching too broadly and affecting tags that start with the same prefix. The issue is clear now: `replaceAll(\"#test\", \"\")` removes all instances of \"#test\" even when it's part of a longer tag like \"#testa\", because the pattern matches as a substring. The fix is to use `replaceFirst()` instead, which only removes the first occurrence of the exact tag text and avoids accidentally matching partial tag names.\n\nNow